I've read the relevant files and haven't edited anything. One thing to know first: commit `510f0b1` already added a signup endpoint, and the newer seed commit `9cf108f` removed it again (from `src/api/account.ts` and `test/signup.test.ts`). So this is a fresh implementation against the current code.

## Plan

**1. `src/api/account.ts`:** add one route to `accountRoutes`. It starts the session by calling `createSession(user.id)` directly, which is what `login()` does after authenticating. I'm not calling `login()` itself, because importing `routes.ts` from `account.ts` would create an import cycle (`routes.ts` already imports `accountRoutes`), and it would hash the password a second time for no reason.

Import changes:
```ts
import { createUser, getUser, publicUser, UserError } from "../auth/users.ts";
import { createSession, revokeSession } from "../auth/session.ts";
```

New entry, added after the `/api/logout` route:
```ts
  {
    method: "POST",
    path: "/api/signup",
    handler: (req) => {
      const email = field(req.body, "email");
      const password = field(req.body, "password");
      if (!email || !password) return json(400, { error: "email and password are required" });
      try {
        const user = createUser(email, field(req.body, "name") ?? "", password);
        const session = createSession(user.id);
        return json(201, { token: session.token, user: publicUser(user) });
      } catch (err) {
        if (err instanceof UserError) return json(400, { error: err.message });
        throw err;
      }
    },
  },
```
The handler checks for a missing email or password up front, the same way login does. Without that check, `createUser` would get `undefined`, throw a `TypeError`, and the router would turn it into a 500. `name` is optional: `createUser` already uses the email as the name when it's blank. Errors other than `UserError` are re-thrown, so the router still returns 500 for them.

**2. `test/signup.test.ts`:** a new file, set up the same way as `test/api.test.ts`:
```ts
import { test, beforeEach } from "node:test";
import assert from "node:assert/strict";
import { app } from "../src/api/routes.ts";
import { resetUsers } from "../src/auth/users.ts";
import { resetSessions } from "../src/auth/session.ts";

const call = (method: string, path: string, body?: unknown, token?: string) =>
  app({ method, path, body, headers: token ? { authorization: `Bearer ${token}` } : {} });

beforeEach(() => {
  resetUsers();
  resetSessions();
});

test("signup creates the user and returns a working session token", async () => {
  const res = await call("POST", "/api/signup", { email: "Grace@Example.com", name: "Grace", password: "hopper1906" });
  assert.equal(res.status, 201);
  const { token, user } = res.body as { token: string; user: { id: string; email: string; name: string } };
  assert.deepEqual(user, { id: user.id, email: "grace@example.com", name: "Grace" });
  const me = await call("GET", "/api/me", undefined, token);
  assert.equal(me.status, 200);
  assert.deepEqual(me.body, user);
});

test("the new account can log in with its password", async () => {
  await call("POST", "/api/signup", { email: "grace@example.com", name: "Grace", password: "hopper1906" });
  const res = await call("POST", "/api/login", { email: "grace@example.com", password: "hopper1906" });
  assert.equal(res.status, 200);
});

test("signup maps UserError to 400", async () => {
  const short = await call("POST", "/api/signup", { email: "grace@example.com", name: "Grace", password: "short" });
